As a youtuber who makes videos about the Earth's environment, and specifically climate,

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:13.440
I receive a lot of comments from people who dispute the influence humanity has on the natural world

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.840
One particular argument which gets brought up again and again is something along the lines of "why haven't sea levels risen?"

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.800
A frequent prediction of anthropogenic climate change is that mean sea levels will rise

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.720
threatening coastal communities, causing mass migration, and apparently the end of the world?

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.320
It certainly doesn't feel like that's happened

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.140
So in this video, let's review the data on global sea levels and work out if humans are actually changing anything

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.580
First of all, let's look at some data from coastal tide gauges, not satellite data,

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:45.580
nothing fancy, an actual physical set of gauges at multiple locations around the world.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.500
Do these measurements show sea level ri-?

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.220
-Oh, wow, yeah, they do

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:55.079
The gauge has shown nearly 25 centimetres of mean sea level rise from 1880 to 2013

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.480
Sea levels have been rising a lot

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:02.250
But the term sea level rise isn't actually terribly useful when talking about climate change

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.300
Because if you look at historical data, from among other things coral cause,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.200
It's clear that mean sea levels have been rising for around 20,000 years

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.420
As this figure suggests, this is a result of the earth coming out of the last glacial maximum,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.860
what you might call the most recent ice age

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.580
About 20,000 years ago, the earth started warming,

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.660
largely due to cyclical variation in its orbit around the Sun, called Milankovitch cycle

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.280
Though there were likely other factors

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.820
As the earth warmed, which we can see very clearly in proxy data like ice cores,

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:35.909
sea levels rose rapidly due to ice sheets melting and pouring into the ocean basins

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:42.599
Water that previously been locked up as ice including a huge amount as ice on land enter the oceans and increase their volume

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.120
when these ice sheets retreated the landscape significantly changed

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.720
making for example, the British Isles separate from mainland Europe

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.600
Returning to the figure, we're very much at the tail end of this interglacial sea level rise

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.820
which appears to be either completely static or decreasing in pace,

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.180
but still changing the mean sea level

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.140
A smart question to ask then is,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.360
"Given this background sea level rise the earth coming out of the last glacial maximum,

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.480
has humanity changed the rate of sea level rise?"

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:17.420
In other words, by influence in the climate through emitting greenhouse gases like co2 have we, as a species, accelerated sea-level rise?

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:22.880
Well, we have a time series of mean sea level stretching back to 1880.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:27.760
The hypothesis is that humans have increased the rate of sea level rise since then

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.740
In terms of this graph, that the rate of sea-level rise (the slope) is greater at the end of the time series than at the start

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.860
Clearly the sea level hasn't risen by the same amount every year. There are plenty of wiggles

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.659
So let's take an average over a long period of time

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.780
Let's compare the average sea level rise in the first 30 years of data to the average sea level rise and the last 30 years of data

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.280
If I was being rigorous,

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.769
Then I'd test if my conclusions were sensitive to the length of time considered,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.440
but I'd leave that as an exercise for the viewer.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.760
In the first 30 years, sea level rose by an average of one point three one millimeters per year

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.560
In the last 30 years, the sea level rose by two point five millimeters per year

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:09.969
So what could have caused this increase? Factors which are known to have caused previous sea level change:

00:03:10.219 --> 00:03:17.649
Milankovitch cycles and continental drift, operate on long timescales and haven't been observed to change significantly over the past century

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:24.560
Another mechanism that could force this increase is variation in solar outputs: the total solar irradiance

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:28.660
Over the past century, this has increased but by less than nought point one percent (0.1%)

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:32.000
not enough to have forced observed acceleration of sea level rise

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:38.540
that leaves one further mechanism known to alter sea levels: changes in radiative forcing

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:43.620
Over the past century, the concentration of radiatively active greenhouse gases in the atmosphere have changed significantly

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.280
due to human emissions, altering the Earth's energy budget

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.820
the resulting increase in global average temperature causes both increased melt of ice sheets like Antarctica and Greenland,

00:03:53.269 --> 00:03:59.408
But also thermal expansion of the oceans. If you heat up water, it expands in volume ever so slightly

00:03:59.408 --> 00:04:09.220
This is well known physics. This isn't noticeable on small scales, but when you expand an entire ocean ever-so-slightly, The results are absolutely measurable.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:18.320
So we have observational evidence that sea level rise has accelerated and that natural forcings have not varied sufficiently on the timescales of observation

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.140
Therefore, we're left to conclude that our hypothesis that humans have accelerated sea level rise is correct

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.560
But this was just me messing around with one time series for 10 minutes

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:36.500
There is an entire scientific literature using lots of data sources including global satellite altimetry

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:43.360
Because sea level rise isn't the same everywhere and more powerful statistical techniques to establish that Yes, sea levels started rising more rapidly

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:50.580
Around the turn of the 20th century and that this rate of change has likely continually increased over the past 100 years

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.920
so we can be pretty sure that humans have accelerated global sea level rise

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.600
but only by a couple of millimeters per year

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.640
That hardly matches up with the predictions of the future looking like Kevin Costner's "Waterworld"

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.560
by which I mean, terrible

